<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#meroitic-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meroitic script</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meroitic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meroitic script was the writing system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> in its Meroitic period, used from about the second century BCE. It existed in hieroglyphic and cursive forms derived from Egyptian writing. Although its signs can be read, the Meroitic language remains only partly understood, and the script is among the Kushite Empire's distinctive legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroitic-script.jpg" alt="Meroitic script" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meroitic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kushites revived pyramid building for their royal tombs, constructing more pyramids at El-Kurru, Nuri and Meroë than were built in Egypt itself, and developed their own <b>Meroitic script</b>, whose signs can be read but whose language is still only partly understood, reflecting growing cultural independence from Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Nubia, conquered by the New Kingdom pharaohs, Egyptian became the language of temples and government, and the kings of Kush who ruled Egypt in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE wrote in Egyptian hieroglyphs; the later <b>Meroitic script</b> of Kush was derived from Egyptian signs.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
